rdfs:comment":"Libraries that hold materials primarily pertaining to asexual people or intended for use by asexual people.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000968","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001755"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002795","dc:identifier":"homoit0002795","skos:prefLabel":"Asexual mothers","rdfs:comment":"Asexual people who are mothers.","skos:broader":["https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001021","https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001762","https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002343"],"skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000421"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002796","dc:identifier":"homoit0002796","skos:prefLabel":"Asexual movement","rdfs:comment":"Umbrella term for activism focused on the rights of asexual people and issues faced by asexual communities.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001026","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001322"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002797","dc:identifier":"homoit0002797","skos:prefLabel":"Asexual newsletters","rdfs:comment":"Serialized print or digital periodicals created by or for asexual people, often with the purpose of sharing news and information related to asexual communities and asexual issues.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001041","skos:related":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002804","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001132"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002798","dc:identifier":"homoit0002798","skos:prefLabel":"Asexual newspapers","rdfs:comment":"Serialized print or digital publications about asexual people or asexual issues (and typically produced by asexual people or asexual organizations) that contain news, articles, columns, correspondence, and advertisements.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001042","skos:related":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002804","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001132"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002799","dc:identifier":"homoit0002799","skos:prefLabel":"Asexual museums","rdfs:comment":"Institutions dedicated to the storage, exhibition, conservation, and preservation of materials relating to asexual people and asexual history.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000907","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001755"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002800","dc:identifier":"homoit0002800","skos:prefLabel":"Asexual partners","rdfs:comment":"Asexual people who are the romantic, sexual, or play partners of another person or people.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001083","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000420"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002801","dc:identifier":"homoit0002801","skos:prefLabel":"Asexual people in the military","rdfs:comment":"Asexual people serving in state and national armed forces.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001100","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000712"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002802","dc:identifier":"homoit0002802","skos:prefLabel":"Asexual people of color","skos:altLabel":"Asexual people of colour","rdfs:comment":"Primarily used in the United States, this term refers to asexual people who are not white and who identify as people of color.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001101","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000915"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002803","dc:identifier":"homoit0002803","skos:prefLabel":"Asexual political activists","rdfs:comment":"Asexual people working to bring about political or social change through public organizing.","skos:broader":["https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000007","https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002758"],"skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000915"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002804","dc:identifier":"homoit0002804","skos:prefLabel":"Asexual press","rdfs:comment":"Newspapers, magazines, or other publishing outlets aimed at asexual audiences and/or operated by asexual people.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001132","skos:narrower":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0003151","skos:related":["https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002797","https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002798"],"skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001132"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002805","dc:identifier":"homoit0002805","skos:prefLabel":"Asexual publishers","rdfs:comment":"Companies and organizations that publish materials for asexual communities or addressing asexual issues.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001144","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001144"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002806","dc:identifier":"homoit0002806","skos:prefLabel":"Asexual radio","rdfs:comment":"Auditory content broadcast on the radio (analog or digital) about asexual people and asexual issues.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001231","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001231"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002807","dc:identifier":"homoit0002807","skos:prefLabel":"Asexual relationships","rdfs:comment":"Ongoing connections (romantic, sexual, or platonic) between two or more asexual people.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001237","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001237"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002808","dc:identifier":"homoit0002808","skos:prefLabel":"Asexual rights","rdfs:comment":"Legal, social, or ethical principles of freedom or entitlement held by asexual people.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000276","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000652"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002809","dc:identifier":"homoit0002809","skos:prefLabel":"LGBTQ+ politicians","skos:altLabel":"LGBTQ+ public officials","rdfs:comment":"LGBTQ+ people who are candidates for public office, are currently holding public office, or have held political positions. Politicians can be elected by public election or otherwise appointed.","skos:narrower":["https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002810","https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002811","https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002812","https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002813","https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002814","https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002815","https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002816"],"skos:related":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002817","skos:exactMatch":{"@id":"http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh2015000368"},"skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002809"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002810","dc:identifier":"homoit0002810","skos:prefLabel":"Gay politicians","skos:altLabel":"Gay public officials","rdfs:comment":"Gay men or mascs who are candidates for public office, are currently holding public office, or have held political positions. Politicians can be elected by public election or otherwise appointed.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002809","skos:exactMatch":{"@id":"http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh91002560"},"skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002809"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002811","dc:identifier":"homoit0002811","skos:prefLabel":"Lesbian politicians","skos:altLabel":"Lesbian public officials","rdfs:comment":"Lesbians who are candidates for public office, are currently holding public office, or have held political positions. Politicians can be elected by public election or otherwise appointed.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002809","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002809"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002812","dc:identifier":"homoit0002812","skos:prefLabel":"Bisexual politicians","skos:altLabel":"Bisexual public officials","rdfs:comment":"Bisexual people who are candidates for public office, are currently holding public office, or have held political positions. Politicians can be elected by public election or otherwise appointed.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002809","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002809"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002813","dc:identifier":"homoit0002813","skos:prefLabel":"Queer politicians","skos:altLabel":"Queer public officials","rdfs:comment":"Queer people who are candidates for public office, are currently holding public office, or have held political positions. Politicians can be elected by public election or otherwise appointed.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002809","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002809"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002814","dc:identifier":"homoit0002814","skos:prefLabel":"Transgender politicians","skos:altLabel":"Transgender public officials","rdfs:comment":"Transgender people who are candidates for public office, are currently holding public office, or have held political positions. Politicians can be elected by public election or otherwise appointed.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002809","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002809"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002815","dc:identifier":"homoit0002815","skos:prefLabel":"Intersex politicians","skos:altLabel":"Intersex public officials","rdfs:comment":"Intersex people who are candidates for public office, are currently holding public office, or have held political positions. Politicians can be elected by public election or otherwise appointed.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002809","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002809"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002816","dc:identifier":"homoit0002816","skos:prefLabel":"Asexual politicians","skos:altLabel":"Asexual public officials","rdfs:comment":"Asexual people who are candidates for public office, are currently holding public office, or have held political positions. Politicians can be elected by public election or otherwise appointed.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002809","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002809"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002817","dc:identifier":"homoit0002817","skos:prefLabel":"Two-Spirit politicians","skos:altLabel":["2S politicians","2S public officials","Two Spirited politicians","Two Spirited public officials","Two-Spirit public officials","Two-Spirited public officials","Two-Spiritied politicians"],"rdfs:comment":"Two-Spirit people who are candidates for public office, are currently holding public office, or have held political positions. Politicians can be elected by public election or otherwise appointed.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001480","skos:related":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002809","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000560"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002818","dc:identifier":"homoit0002818","skos:prefLabel":"17-beta hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase 3 deficiency","skos:altLabel":"17-beta","rdfs:comment":"People with 17-beta hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase 3 deficiency, abbreviated as \"17-beta,\" have XY chromosomes and testes, but their bodies do not have one of the enzymes needed to synthesize testosterone. Most people with 17-beta are born with a vulva and vagina, and their testes are most often undescended. Some people with 17-beta will be born with genitals that do not clearly resemble a typical penis or a typical vulva. Some will have a penis that may be smaller than typical (known as a micropenis), and the urethra may open on the underside of the penis rather than at the tip (known as hypospadias). In adolescence, people with 17-beta will often develop some secondary sex characteristics associated with a typical testosterone puberty.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000669","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001269"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002819","dc:identifier":"homoit0002819","skos:prefLabel":"Ambiguous genitalia","rdfs:comment":"A term used to describe a birth variation in which the outer genitals do not have the typical appearance of those associated with either boys or girls. This is often the first sign many doctors and parents see that indicate their child has an intersex variation. Ambiguous genitalia can be caused by a variety of variations and rarely require immediate surgical intervention as long as the child is able to excrete and urinate without complications. Many intersex people find this term stigmatizing, so it should not be used unless the individual uses it to describe themselves.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000669","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001269"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002820","dc:identifier":"homoit0002820","skos:prefLabel":"Androgens","rdfs:comment":"A term that encompasses testosterone, dihydrotestosterone, and other hormones primarily produced in the testes but also in the ovaries and adrenal glands.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000651","skos:related":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000317","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001269"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002821","dc:identifier":"homoit0002821","skos:prefLabel":"Anorchia","rdfs:comment":"A person with anorchia has 46 XY chromosomes and is born without testes. As a result, they do not produce typical levels of testosterone. Someone with anorchia will usually have a typical-appearing penis and scrotum because these organs are already formed by the stage of fetal development when the testes disappear.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000669","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001269"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002822","dc:identifier":"homoit0002822","skos:prefLabel":"Aromatase deficiency","rdfs:comment":"People with aromatase deficiency do not have the enzyme responsible for converting androgens into estrogen. At birth, someone with aromatase deficiency and XX chromosomes may have a larger-than-typical clitoris and partially or completely fused labia (resembling the appearance of a scrotum). At puberty, they may not develop breasts, may not begin menstruating, and may develop some secondary sex characteristics associated with a typical testosterone puberty. They may also develop multiple cysts on their ovaries. People with XY chromosomes can also have aromatase deficiency, but they do not typically experience variations in their sex characteristics, though they may grow taller than is typical because estrogen is usually responsible for closing the bone growth plates.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000669","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001269"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002823","dc:identifier":"homoit0002823","skos:prefLabel":"Aromatase excess syndrome","skos:altLabel":["Familial gynecomastia","Hyperestrogenism "],"rdfs:comment":"People with aromatase excess have increased production of aromatase, which converts androgens (like testosterone) into estrogen. People with XY chromosomes and aromatase excess syndrome will therefore have more active estrogen in their bodies than is typical. As a result, they will often stop growing at a shorter height and/or develop breasts in adolescence along with features associated with a typical testosterone puberty. People with XX chromosomes can also have aromatase excess, but it doesn't usually cause their sex characteristics to vary from what is considered typical for those assigned female at birth, though they may also experience breast enlargement and short stature, along with menstrual irregularities.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000669","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001269"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002824","dc:identifier":"homoit0002824","skos:prefLabel":"Bladder exstrophy","rdfs:comment":"People with bladder exstrophy are born with their urinary bladder exposed (through an open abdominal wall) or outside of the body. They may also have an open pelvis (resulting from the pubic bones not joining together), which causes genital differences. People with bladder exstrophy and XY chromosomes often have a shorter-than-typical penis with epispadias (meaning the urethra opens on the top surface of the penis instead of at the tip) and undescended testes. People with XX chromosomes and bladder exstrophy often have a urethra that is placed higher than usual, widely spaced labia (due to the wide spacing of their pubic bones), and a clitoris formed in two halves with a split in the middle.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000669","skos:exactMatch":{"@id":"http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85014741"},"skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001269"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002825","dc:identifier":"homoit0002825","skos:prefLabel":"Chimeras","skos:altLabel":"Human chimeras","rdfs:comment":"Chimeras, or people with chimerism, have two different sets of DNA. This happens when a person is pregnant with fraternal twins, but the embryos combine into one fetus.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000618","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000915"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002826","dc:identifier":"homoit0002826","skos:prefLabel":"Chimerism","rdfs:comment":"Term used to describe the variation in which a person is born with two or more different sets of DNA. This can occur when multiple zygotes (such as fraternal twins) combine and develop into one fetus. Sex-chromosome discordant chimerism occurs when at least one embryo with XX chromosomes fuses with at least one embryo with XY chromosomes. A person who develops this way would have sets of both XX and XY chromosomes, or a chromosome pattern of 46XX/46XY, and could have a wide variety of different possible sex characteristics. Many people with chimerism and XX/XY chromosomes have both ovarian and testicular tissue, while others have only testes or only ovaries, and still others have gonadal dysgenesis. Some have noticeable genital differences and others do not.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000669","skos:closeMatch":{"@id":"http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh2011001809"},"skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001269"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002827","dc:identifier":"homoit0002827","skos:prefLabel":"Classical congenital adrenal hyperplasia","rdfs:comment":"People who have classical congenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH), a subtype of CAH, have more pronounced issues with cortisol and/or aldosterone production. This variation is likely to be noticed at birth or shortly after.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000301","skos:narrower":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002874","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001269"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002828","dc:identifier":"homoit0002828","skos:prefLabel":"Cloacal exstrophy","rdfs:comment":"People born with cloacal exstrophy are born with an open abdominal wall exposing their colon, bladder, and sometimes other abdominal organs. Their organs may also be positioned outside of the body at birth. The bladder is often split into two halves, and the colon and bladder may be connected. The anal opening may be blocked or not have been formed. The pubic bones do not join together, resulting in an open pelvis with noticeable genital differences. People with XY chromosomes and cloacal exstrophy may have a smaller-than-typical penis that is flat or split into two halves with an epispadic urethra (opening on the top of the penis rather than at the tip) and undescended testes. Someone with XX chromosomes and cloacal exstrophy usually has a clitoris that is split into two halves, and they may have two vaginal openings.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000669","skos:exactMatch":{"@id":"http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh90003339"},"skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001269"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002829","dc:identifier":"homoit0002829","skos:prefLabel":"Complete androgen insensitivity syndrome","skos:altLabel":"CAIS","rdfs:comment":"People with complete androgen insensitivity syndrome (CAIS) have no response to androgens and are born with a vulva and clitoris, as well as a shorter-than-typical vagina and undescended testes. In adolescence, they will develop breasts and other features associated with a typical estrogen puberty due to the body’s natural conversion of testosterone to estrogen. However, people with CAIS do not menstruate because they do not have a uterus or ovaries.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000669","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001269"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002830","dc:identifier":"homoit0002830","skos:prefLabel":"Complete gonadal dysgenesis","skos:altLabel":"Pure gonadal dysgenesis","rdfs:comment":"People with complete gonadal dysgenesis are born with XX or XY chromosomes and gonadal tissue that has not developed into testicles or ovaries (also known as streak gonads). They are generally born with a vulva and vagina, and they will not usually develop secondary sex characteristics at puberty because streak gonads do not produce hormones.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000044","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001269"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002831","dc:identifier":"homoit0002831","skos:prefLabel":"Congenital chordee","rdfs:comment":"People born with congenital chordee have a penis that curves upward or downward or bends to one side, usually due to bands of fibrous tissue that pull the penis in a particular direction. People with congenital chordee often have hypospadias, meaning that their urethral opening is not at the tip of the penis.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000669","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001269"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002832","dc:identifier":"homoit0002832","skos:prefLabel":"Cryptorchidism","skos:altLabel":["Undescended testes","Undescended testicles"],"rdfs:comment":"An intersex variation in which one or both of the testes do not descend from the abdomen into the scrotum. If both testes are undescended, this is known as \"bilateral cryptorchidism,\" whereas \"unilateral cryptorchidism\" occurs when only one the testes does not descend.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000669","skos:exactMatch":{"@id":"http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85034467"},"skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001269"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002833","dc:identifier":"homoit0002833","skos:prefLabel":"De la Chapelle syndrome","skos:altLabel":"XX male syndrome","rdfs:comment":"De la Chapelle syndrome causes people with XX chromosomes to be born with a penis and testes. This usually happens because a particular gene typically seen on Y chromosomes (known as the SRY gene) ends up on one of their X chromosomes and causes their genitals and internal reproductive organs to develop as they typically would in someone with XY chromosomes. People with de la Chapelle might have testes that are smaller than typical or that are undescended, and they are often infertile. In adolescence, they may experience breast growth, and they might not develop the characteristics that are usually associated with a typical testosterone puberty.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000669","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001269"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002835","dc:identifier":"homoit0002835","skos:prefLabel":"Early genital surgery model","skos:altLabel":"John Money model","rdfs:comment":"Patient care model developed by John Money, a New Zealand psychologist who theorized that gender was completely dependent on \"nurture.\" This model advocates for performing genital surgery on intersex infants and raising them as girls. Money's theories have resulted in many surgically misgendered children who have had to deal with life-long gender dysphoria, depression, sexual dysfunction, bowel and urinary incontinence, and nerve pain. A patient-centered model, in which the intersex child is allowed to grow up and make their own choices regarding their gender and surgical option, is now preferred.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000352","skos:related":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001006","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001269"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002836","dc:identifier":"homoit0002836","skos:prefLabel":"Epispadias","skos:altLabel":"Anaspadias","rdfs:comment":"People with epispadias are born with a urinary opening located on the upper surface of their penis rather than at the tip (similar to the more common variation hypospadias). People with vulvas can also be born with epispadias, in which case the urethral opening is wider and located higher than typical. The clitoris may also be split into two halves. Epispadias is usually present whenever bladder exstrophy or cloacal exstrophy develop, but epispadias can also occur by itself.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000669","skos:exactMatch":{"@id":"http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85044450"},"skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001269"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002837","dc:identifier":"homoit0002837","skos:prefLabel":"Estrogen insensitivity syndrome","skos:altLabel":"EIS","rdfs:comment":"People with estrogen insensitivity syndrome (EIS) have a genetic variation in which their bodies do not respond to estrogen. People with XX chromosomes and EIS are usually born with a vulva and vagina, may have a small uterus and enlarged ovaries that later develop multiple cysts, and may produce very high levels of estrogen that their bodies do not recognize. In adolescence, they will most likely not experience the changes associated with a typical estrogen puberty such as breast development or menstruation. They may instead develop pubic hair and acne due to the influence of androgens. People with XY chromosomes can also be born with EIS, and they may or may not experience variations in their other hormone production, testicular development, and development of secondary sex characteristics as a result.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000669","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001269"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002838","dc:identifier":"homoit0002838","skos:prefLabel":"Follicle-stimulating hormone insensitivity","skos:altLabel":["FSH ","FSH insensitivity"],"rdfs:comment":"People with follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) insensitivity have a genetic variation where their bodies do not respond to FSH. People with XX chromosomes and FSH insensitivity will usually be born with a vulva, vagina, and ovaries. Their ovaries may produce lower amounts of estrogen than is considered typical, and they may not produce fertile follicles (eggs). In adolescence, they may not develop secondary sex characteristics associated with a typical estrogen puberty and may not menstruate. People with XY chromosomes and FSH insensitivity may develop smaller-than-typical testes, may produce fewer sperm than usual, and may not be fertile.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000669","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001269"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002839","dc:identifier":"homoit0002839","skos:prefLabel":"Fraser syndrome","rdfs:comment":"People with Fraser syndrome are usually born with differences in the development of their eyelids, fingers, and toes. They may also have variations in their sex characteristics, such as a larger-than-typical clitoris or undescended testes. They may develop without one or both kidneys, and other parts of the urinary system can additionally be affected.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000669","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001269"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002840","dc:identifier":"homoit0002840","skos:prefLabel":"Gonadectomy","rdfs:comment":"Surgical procedure to remove the gonads, including testes and ovaries.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000584","skos:closeMatch":{"@id":"http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85020752"},"skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001004"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002841","dc:identifier":"homoit0002841","skos:prefLabel":"Hopkins model","skos:altLabel":"Concealment centered model","rdfs:comment":"A patient care model that gained popular usage in the 1950s. In this model, parents are used to quickly decide on a gender assignment for their intersex baby, under the assumption that the child would identify with whichever gender their parents raised them as. If a child later deviated from the assigned gender, parents were encouraged to verbally and physically \"correct\" the child and enforce gender norms. In practice, this resulted in life-altering, unnecessary genital surgeries, irreversible hormone treatments, and dishonesty on the parent's part. A patient-centered model, in which the intersex child is allowed to grow up and make their own choices regarding their gender and surgical option, is now preferred. ","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000352","skos:related":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001006","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001269"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002842","dc:identifier":"homoit0002842","skos:prefLabel":"Hyperandrogenism","rdfs:comment":"People with hyperandrogenism have higher-than-typical levels of testosterone and/or other androgens. In people with XX chromosomes, hyperandrogenism can result in the development of secondary sex characteristics that are associated with the influence of testosterone (such as facial and body hair) to varying degrees. Hyperandrogenism may or may not be the result of an underlying intersex variation, but it is common in several variations (including CAH and PCOS). People with hyperandrogenism who aren’t intersex will nonetheless often have many experiences in common with intersex people, such as encountering exclusion and discrimination in sex-segregated contexts like sports.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000669","skos:exactMatch":{"@id":"http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh97002310"},"skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001269"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002843","dc:identifier":"homoit0002843","skos:prefLabel":"Hypogonadism","rdfs:comment":"People with hypogonadism produce lower-than-typical levels of hormones like testosterone and estrogen, or none at all. This can affect how a person’s secondary sex characteristics develop as well as their fertility. Hypogonadism can be a result of a difference in how someone’s brain signals for their gonads to produce (or not produce) hormones, or it can be a feature of their gonads directly. Hypogonadism is not always caused by an intersex variation, but can be related to Klinefelter syndrome, Turner syndrome, and several other variations.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000669","skos:exactMatch":{"@id":"http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85063790"},"skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001269"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002844","dc:identifier":"homoit0002844","skos:prefLabel":"Informed consent","rdfs:comment":"The practice of weighing all options and considering evidence, alternatives, and potential risks in order to make a truly informed medical decision. Such decisions are best made over a period of time rather than under pressure.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000302","skos:related":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001004","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000302"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002846","dc:identifier":"homoit0002846","skos:prefLabel":"Intersexual","skos:altLabel":"Intersex ","rdfs:comment":"This term is not common in the English language as to not confuse intersex with a gender identity or sex orientation by adding \"ual\" but it is used in other languages such as Spanish to mean \"intersex.\" ","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002846"},"dc:isReplacedBy":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000352"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-12-08"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002847","dc:identifier":"homoit0002847","skos:prefLabel":"Intersex surgery","rdfs:comment":"Non-lifesaving cosmetic procedures intended to change and \"normalize\" natural variations in genital appearance and/or reproductive anatomy. Intersex surgery was first developed in the early 20th century, with doctors making unresearched claims about an \"urgent need\" for these operations.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000670","skos:related":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000584","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000670"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002848","dc:identifier":"homoit0002848","skos:prefLabel":"Isolated 17,20-lyase deficiency","skos:altLabel":"ILD","rdfs:comment":"People with isolated 17,20-lyase deficiency (ILD) have one of several specific variations in their genes that cause their bodies to make lower-than-typical amounts of testosterone. They may be born with genitals that do not appear quite like a typical penis, and their testes may be descended or undescended. In adolescence, they may not experience all of the changes related to a typical testosterone puberty because their variation impacts their hormone production.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000669","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001269"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002849","dc:identifier":"homoit0002849","skos:prefLabel":"Asexual studies","rdfs:comment":"Academic field of research and study about Asexual people, Asexual identity, and Asexual communities.","skos:related":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001210","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002849"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002850","dc:identifier":"homoit0002850","skos:prefLabel":"Asexual survivors of hate crimes","rdfs:comment":"Asexual people who have experienced and survived hate crimes; follow the individual's preference for \"victims\" or \"survivors\" whenever possible.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001349","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000915"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002851","dc:identifier":"homoit0002851","skos:prefLabel":"Asexual survivors of rape","rdfs:comment":"Asexual people who have experienced and survived rape; follow the individual's preference for \"victims\" or \"survivors\" whenever possible.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001350","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000915"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002852","dc:identifier":"homoit0002852","skos:prefLabel":"Asexual survivors of sexual abuse","rdfs:comment":"Asexual people who have experienced and survived sexual abuse; follow the individual's preference for \"victims\" or \"survivors\" whenever possible.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001351","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000915"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002853","dc:identifier":"homoit0002853","skos:prefLabel":"Asexual survivors of war","rdfs:comment":"Asexual people who have experienced and survived war; follow the individual's preference for \"victims\" or \"survivors\" whenever possible.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001352","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000915"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002854","dc:identifier":"homoit0002854","skos:prefLabel":"Asexual television","rdfs:comment":"Television shows featuring asexual characters or themes.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001359","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001359"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002855","dc:identifier":"homoit0002855","skos:prefLabel":"Asexual victims of rape","rdfs:comment":"Asexual people who have experienced rape and may or may not have survived; follow the individual's preference for \"victims\" or \"survivors\" whenever possible.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000958","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000915"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002856","dc:identifier":"homoit0002856","skos:prefLabel":"Asexual victims of sexual abuse","rdfs:comment":"Asexual people who have experienced sexual abuse and may or may not have survived; follow the individual's preference for \"victims\" or \"survivors\" whenever possible.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000959","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000915"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002857","dc:identifier":"homoit0002857","skos:prefLabel":"Asexual victims of war","rdfs:comment":"Asexual people who have experienced war and may or may not have survived; follow the individual's preference for \"victims\" or \"survivors\" whenever possible.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000961","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000915"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002858","dc:identifier":"homoit0002858","skos:prefLabel":"Asexual victims' rights","rdfs:comment":"Asexual people’s legal rights to be informed, present, and heard within the criminal justice system after having been harmed or injured as the result of a crime, accident, or other event.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001492","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000702"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002859","dc:identifier":"homoit0002859","skos:prefLabel":"Asexual-focused businesses","rdfs:comment":"Businesses focusing on asexual clientele and products related to asexual communities.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001741","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001741"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002860","dc:identifier":"homoit0002860","skos:prefLabel":"Asexual-owned businesses","rdfs:comment":"Businesses owned by asexual people.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001735","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001735"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002861","dc:identifier":"homoit0002861","skos:prefLabel":"Jacobs syndrome","skos:altLabel":["47XYY","XYY syndrome"],"rdfs:comment":"People with Jacobs syndrome develop with an extra Y chromosome. They might not have any obvious physical variations as a result, or they may be taller than usual or have other identifiable differences that do not relate to sex characteristics. Some people with Jacobs syndrome may also have a smaller-than-typical penis or scrotum, hypospadias, and/or undescended testicles.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000669","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001269"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002862","dc:identifier":"homoit0002862","skos:prefLabel":"Kallmann syndrome","skos:altLabel":"Hypogonadotropic hypogonadism","rdfs:comment":"Kallmann syndrome can occur in people with XX or XY chromosomes and is a form of hypogonadism. People with Kallmann syndrome and XY chromosomes are often born with a smaller-than-typical penis and undescended testes. In adolescence, most people with Kallmann syndrome will either start puberty later than usual or not experience typical pubertal changes (such as developing facial hair and a deeper voice for people with XY chromosomes or developing breasts and starting to menstruate for people with XX chromosomes) unless they receive hormone therapy. Kallmann syndrome also affects someone’s sense of smell.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000669","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001269"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002863","dc:identifier":"homoit0002863","skos:prefLabel":"Leydig cell hypoplasia","skos:altLabel":"LCH","rdfs:comment":"People with Leydig cell hypoplasia (LCH) have XY chromosomes and a genetic insensitivity to luteinizing hormone. LCH can be either complete (known as LCH Type 1) or partial (known as LCH Type 2). Luteinizing hormone is typically responsible for the development of Leydig cells in the testicles and for signaling for those cells to produce androgens like testosterone. Someone with LCH will develop few or no Leydig cells and therefore will produce less testosterone than is typical.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000669","skos:narrower":["https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002879","https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002881"],"skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001269"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002864","dc:identifier":"homoit0002864","skos:prefLabel":"Luteinizing hormone insensitivity","rdfs:comment":"People with XY chromosomes and luteinizing hormone insensitivity develop the variation Leydig cell hypoplasiae. Because people with XX chromosomes do not typically produce Leydig cells to begin with, someone with XX chromosomes who has a genetic insensitivity to luteinizing hormone will not be affected in the same way. A person with XX chromosomes whose body does not respond to luteinizing hormone will not usually have any noticeable differences at birth, but their ovaries may not produce fertile follicles (eggs), and they may not menstruate.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000669","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001269"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002865","dc:identifier":"homoit0002865","skos:prefLabel":"Mayer-Rokitansky-Küster-Hauser syndrome","skos:altLabel":["MRKH syndrome ","Müllerian agenesis","Müllerian duct anomalies","Müllerian duct variations"],"rdfs:comment":"Mayer-Rokitansky-Küster-Hauser (MRKH) syndrome occurs when a person's Müllerian ducts, which typically become the uterus and upper portion of the vagina during fetal development, do not develop in the typical way. People with MRKH have XX chromosomes and are usually born with a vulva and either a vagina that is shorter than typical or no vagina. They usually do not have a cervix and may have a partial uterus (uterine remnant) or no uterus. They often do not mensturate but may experience cyclic pain if they have a uterine remnant with endometrial lining, and they can develop menstruation-related conditions like endometriosis.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000669","skos:narrower":["https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002880","https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002882"],"skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001269"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002866","dc:identifier":"homoit0002866","skos:prefLabel":"Mild androgen insensitivity syndrome","skos:altLabel":"MAIS","rdfs:comment":"People with mild androgen insensitivity syndrome (MAIS) respond to testosterone at a somewhat reduced level compared to typical levels, which means that they will usually be born with a penis (which may or may not be smaller than typical) and will later develop secondary sex characteristics associated with a typical testosterone puberty. Because their bodies convert a small amount of their naturally produced androgens into estrogen, they may have visible variations in features, such as the amount of facial hair, body hair, or muscle tone they develop. They may also experience some breast development during puberty.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000044","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001269"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002867","dc:identifier":"homoit0002867","skos:prefLabel":"Mixed gonadal dysgenesis","skos:altLabel":"Partial gonadal dysgenesis","rdfs:comment":"People with mixed gonadal dysgenesis are born with a mosaic (usually 45X/46XY) chromosome pattern and may develop some gonadal streak tissue and testicular tissue. They may be born with a vulva or penis, or with visible genital variations. They usually have a partially or completely developed uterus, and they may have combinations of internal reproductive structures such as a fallopian tube and a vas deferens on opposite sides of the body. In adolescence, people with mixed gonadal dysgenesis may develop some secondary sex characteristics associated with a typical testosterone puberty, depending on the amount of hormone-producing testicular tissue they have.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000599","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001269"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002868","dc:identifier":"homoit0002868","skos:prefLabel":"Müllerian duct variations","skos:altLabel":["MRKH ","Müllerian agenesis","Müllerian duct anomalies"],"rdfs:comment":"People with XX chromosomes can have a broad range of variations in how their Müllerian ducts (which typically form the uterus, fallopian tubes, cervix, and upper portion of the vagina) develop. They might have a variation that causes their uterus to develop with a different shape than typical, or to be doubled. Others may have vaginal variations such as a “hemivagina” (where one side of the vagina is obstructed, which can sometimes block menstrual blood from exiting), or a band of tissue dividing the vaginal canal (known as a vaginal septum). Müllerian duct variations usually don’t cause someone to have any variations in their ovaries or hormone production.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000669","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001269"},"dc:isReplacedBy":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002865"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2024-01-30"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002869","dc:identifier":"homoit0002869","skos:prefLabel":"Non-classical congenital adrenal hyperplasia","skos:altLabel":"Late-onset congenital adrenal hyperplasia","rdfs:comment":"Non-classical congenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH), a subtype of CAH, does not usually become apparent until later in childhood, adolescence, or even young adulthood.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000301","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001269"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002870","dc:identifier":"homoit0002870","skos:prefLabel":"Penoscrotal transposition","rdfs:comment":"People with penoscrotal transposition are born with a different genital configuration than typical, with their penis located below or in the middle of their scrotum instead of above it. Their scrotum might be bifid (split into two halves), and they may also have hypospadias and/or chordee.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000669","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001269"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002871","dc:identifier":"homoit0002871","skos:prefLabel":"Persistent Müllerian duct syndrome","skos:altLabel":"PMDS","rdfs:comment":"People with Persistent Müllerian duct syndrome (PMDS) have XY chromosomes, male-typical reproductive organs and external genitalia, and some component(s) of a female-typical reproductive tract, such as a uterus, fallopian tubes, and/or upper vaginal canal. PMDS occurs when the Müllerian ducts—internal structures that ordinarily break down in an XY fetus—remain and develop as they would in an XX fetus. PMDS is ordinarily not diagnosed at birth, and individuals with this variation often have a male gender identity.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000669","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001269"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002872","dc:identifier":"homoit0002872","skos:prefLabel":"Polycystic ovary syndrome","skos:altLabel":["PCOD","PCOS","Polycystic ovarian disease","Polycystic ovarian syndrome"],"rdfs:comment":"People with this variation have XX chromosomes and are generally born with a vulva and vagina, a uterus, and ovaries. Later in life, their ovaries often produce higher-than-typical levels of androgens like testosterone. Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) can also result in the development of characteristics such as facial and body hair, and it can interfere with the process of ovulation. This in turn can cause irregular periods or no periods, and people with PCOS may struggle to become pregnant. Some people with PCOS have small cysts (fluid-filled sacs) on their ovaries, but some do not. Signs of PCOS generally will not show up until a person starts menstruating, or later in life.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000669","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001269"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002873","dc:identifier":"homoit0002873","skos:prefLabel":"Pseudohermaphroditism","skos:altLabel":["Female pseudohermaphroditism","Male pseudohermaphroditism"],"rdfs:comment":"A term previously used in medical contexts to refer to different groups of intersex variations, usually divided into categories of “male pseudohermaphroditism” and “female pseudohermaphroditism” on the basis of the person’s chromosomes. People may still find these terms used in their medical records or in medical journal articles when researching their variation, but they are often considered highly stigmatizing in addition to being vague and inaccurate. Do not use this term to describe a person's variation unless they do so themselves.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000669","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001269"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002874","dc:identifier":"homoit0002874","skos:prefLabel":"Salt-wasting congenital adrenal hyperplasia","rdfs:comment":"People who have salt-wasting congenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH), a subtype of classical CAH, experience \"salt-wasting,\" a dangerous scenario a person's low levels of aldosterone cause their body to lose too much sodium. They may experience life-threatening adrenal crises, especially when their body is under stress.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002827","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001269"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002875","dc:identifier":"homoit0002875","skos:prefLabel":"Sex reversal syndrome","rdfs:comment":"A term sometimes used in medical contexts to refer to different variations in which a person is born with XY chromosomes and a vulva and vagina or XX chromosomes and a penis.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000669","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001269"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002876","dc:identifier":"homoit0002876","skos:prefLabel":"Steroidogenic factor 1 protein","skos:altLabel":"SF-1 protein","rdfs:comment":"The steroidogenic factor 1 (SF-1) protein is a transcription factor involved in sex determination. The SF-1 protein controls the activity of genes related to the gonads and adrenal glands.","skos:related":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000651","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002876"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002877","dc:identifier":"homoit0002877","skos:prefLabel":"Streak gonads","rdfs:comment":"Minimally developed gonadal tissue that is present in place of either testes or ovaries. Streak gonads are more common in certain intersex variations such as Swyer syndrome.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000600","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001269"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002878","dc:identifier":"homoit0002878","skos:prefLabel":"Trisomy X","skos:altLabel":"Triple XXX syndrome","rdfs:comment":"People with Trisomy X are born with 47XXX chromosomes rather than the typical 46XX. If they receive the additional X chromosome from their parent's original sperm or egg cell, they will have 47XXX chromosomes in all their cells. If the additional chromosome appears during the embryo's development process, however, only some cells will have 47XXX chromosomes and the person will be born with a mosaic chromosome pattern. Most people with 47XXX chromosomes do not develop any variations in their other sex characteristics but some may have ovaries that stop producing hormones at an earlier-than-typical age and some may not menstruate.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000669","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001269"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002879","dc:identifier":"homoit0002879","skos:prefLabel":"Type 1 Leydig cell hypoplasia","skos:altLabel":"Type 1 LCH","rdfs:comment":"People with Type 1 Leydig cell hypoplasia (LCH) produce almost no testosterone and will usually be born with a vulva and vagina and undescended testes. They are usually assigned female at birth, often without their variation being recognized until adolescence, at which point they generally will not develop any secondary sex characteristics or experience other changes typically associated with puberty.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002863","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001269"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002880","dc:identifier":"homoit0002880","skos:prefLabel":"Type 1 Mayer-Rokitansky-Küster-Hauser syndrome","skos:altLabel":"Type 1 MRKH","rdfs:comment":"People with Type 1 Mayer-Rokitansky-Küster-Hauser (MRKH) syndrome typically have only the differences associated with MRKH syndrome.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002865","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001269"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002881","dc:identifier":"homoit0002881","skos:prefLabel":"Type 2 Leydig cell hypoplasia","skos:altLabel":"Type 2 LCH","rdfs:comment":"People with Type 2 Leydig cell hypoplasia (LCH) have some response to luteinizing hormone and produce lower-than-typical amounts of testosterone, but more than people with Type 1 LCH. At birth, their testes may be descended or undescended, and they may have a smaller-than-typical penis, often with other noticeable differences such as hypospadias. In adolescence, they will often develop some characteristics associated with a typical testosterone puberty.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002863","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001269"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002882","dc:identifier":"homoit0002882","skos:prefLabel":"Type 2 Mayer-Rokitansky-Küster-Hauser syndrome","skos:altLabel":"Type 2 MRKH","rdfs:comment":"People with Type 2 Mayer-Rokitansky-Küster-Hauser (MRKH) syndrome have both the differences associated with MRKH syndrome and additional differences in other parts of their body, such as their fallopian tubes, kidneys, spine, or heart. A specific example of Type 2 MRKH known as Müllerian agenesis, Renal agenesis, Cervicothoracic Somite (MURCS) association, occurs when someone’s kidneys do not develop typically. They may have scoliosis or fused vertebrae in addition to the usual features of MRKH. ","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002865","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001269"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002883","dc:identifier":"homoit0002883","skos:prefLabel":"Vaginal atresia","rdfs:comment":"People with this variation may be born without a vaginal opening, a shallow “dimple” where an opening would typically be, or a vaginal opening that is fused or blocked by fibrous tissue. People with vaginal atresia often have a typical upper vaginal canal (along with a typical uterus, cervix, fallopian tubes, and ovaries) with differences in the lower portion of the vagina only.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000669","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001269"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002884","dc:identifier":"homoit0002884","skos:prefLabel":"X gender markers","rdfs:comment":"Gender markers that indicate a person is NOT cisgender. X gender markers may be assigned to or adopted by intersex people or transgender people.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000573","skos:related":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001646","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000712"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002885","dc:identifier":"homoit0002885","skos:prefLabel":"XXYY syndrome","rdfs:comment":"People with XXYY syndrome are born with an extra copy of both an X and Y chromosome, resulting in an 48XXYY chromosome pattern instead of the typical 46XY. People with XXYY chromosomes may have undescended testes and produce lower-than-typical amounts of testosterone, which can cause them to start puberty at a later age than typical and develop less pronounced secondary sex characteristics like muscle tone and facial hair. They may also experience breast growth. People with XXYY syndrome often have other physical features that are similar to those developed by people with Klinefelter syndrome (47XXY).","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000669","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001269"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002886","dc:identifier":"homoit0002886","skos:prefLabel":"LGBTQ+ operas ","rdfs:comment":"Long-form musical performances combining singing, orchestral music, and often ballet, typically set to a dramatic narrative or libretto and presented on a theater stage centered on LGBTQ+ characters or themes.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000816","skos:narrower":["https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002887","https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002888","https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002889","https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002890","https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002891","https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0003166","https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0003167"],"skos:related":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0003168","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001345"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002887","dc:identifier":"homoit0002887","skos:prefLabel":"Gay operas","rdfs:comment":"Operas created by gay directors, depicting gay men, or that are regarded as important within gay culture.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002886","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001345"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002888","dc:identifier":"homoit0002888","skos:prefLabel":"Bisexual operas","rdfs:comment":"Operas created by bisexual directors, depicting bisexual individuals, or that are regarded as important within bisexual culture.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002886","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001345"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002889","dc:identifier":"homoit0002889","skos:prefLabel":"Transgender operas","rdfs:comment":"Operas created by transgender directors, depicting transgender individuals, or that are regarded as important within transgender culture.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002886","skos:related":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0003168","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001345"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002890","dc:identifier":"homoit0002890","skos:prefLabel":"Asexual operas","rdfs:comment":"Operas created by asexual directors, depicting asexual individuals, or that are regarded as important within asexual culture.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002886","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001345"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002891","dc:identifier":"homoit0002891","skos:prefLabel":"Intersex operas","rdfs:comment":"Operas created by intersex directors, depicting intersex individuals, or that are regarded as important within intersex culture.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002886","skos:related":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0003168","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001345"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002892","dc:identifier":"homoit0002892","skos:prefLabel":"Aboriginal Australian asexual people","skos:altLabel":["Aboriginal asexual people","Asexual Aboriginal Australian people","Asexual Aboriginal Australians"],"rdfs:comment":"Asexual people who belong to one or more of the various Indigenous peoples of Australia; do not use to refer to the Indigenous people of the Torres Strait Islands, who are considered ethnically and culturally distinct. Only use to describe people who identify as Aboriginal Australian.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000002","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000915"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002893","dc:identifier":"homoit0002893","skos:prefLabel":"Afro-Canadian asexual people","skos:altLabel":["Asexual Afro-Canadian people","Asexual Afro-Canadians"],"rdfs:comment":"Asexual people of African descent born in Canada or asexual people born in African countries who live in or have a strong connection to Canada. Use for individuals who self-identify as Afro-Canadian.","skos:broader":["https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000016","https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002334"],"skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000915"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-29"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002894","dc:identifier":"homoit0002894","skos:prefLabel":"Afro-Caribbean asexual people","skos:altLabel":["Asexual Afro-Caribbean people","Asexual Afro-Caribbeans"],"rdfs:comment":"Asexual people of African descent born in the Caribbean or asexual people born in African countries who live in or have a strong connection to the Caribbean. Only use for individuals who self-identify as Afro-Caribbean.","skos:broader":["https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000017","https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002334"],"skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000915"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-29"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002895","dc:identifier":"homoit0002895","skos:prefLabel":"Asexual victims of hate crimes","rdfs:comment":"Asexual people who have experienced hate crimes and may or may not have survived; follow the individual's preference for \"victims\" or \"survivors\" whenever possible.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000613","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000915"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-29"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002896","dc:identifier":"homoit0002896","skos:prefLabel":"Afro-European asexual people","skos:altLabel":["Asexual Afro-European people","Asexual Afro-Europeans"],"rdfs:comment":"Asexual people of African descent born in European countries or asexual people born in African countries who live in or have a strong connection to Europe. Only use for individuals who self-identify as Afro-European.","skos:broader":["https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000018","https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002334"],"skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000915"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-29"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002897","dc:identifier":"homoit0002897","skos:prefLabel":"Latino/a/x asexual people","skos:altLabel":["Asexual Latinas ","Asexual Latine people","Asexual Latinos","Asexual Latinx people","Latina asexual people","Latine asexual people","Latino asexual people","Latinx asexual people"],"rdfs:comment":"Latin American people who are asexual; only use to describe individuals who self-identify as Latino, Latina, or Latinx. Do not conflate with Chicano/a/x or Hispanic.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000253","skos:narrower":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002898","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000915"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-29"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002898","dc:identifier":"homoit0002898","skos:prefLabel":"Afro-Latin American asexual people","skos:altLabel":"Asexual Afro-Latin Americans","rdfs:comment":"Asexual people of African descent born in Latin American countries or asexual people born in African countries who live in or have a strong connection to Latin America. Only use for individuals who self-identify as Afro-Latin American.","skos:broader":["https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000019","https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002334","https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002897"],"skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000915"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-29"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002899","dc:identifier":"homoit0002899","skos:prefLabel":"Anti-asexual discrimination","rdfs:comment":"The unjust or prejudicial treatment of asexual people due to their sexual identity; may include the denial of legal rights, employment, and housing.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000356","skos:related":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000112","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000356"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-29"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002900","dc:identifier":"homoit0002900","skos:prefLabel":"Anti-asexual violence","rdfs:comment":"Behavior involving physical force intended to harm or kill asexual people on the basis of their identity.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000059","skos:related":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000109","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000059"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-29"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002901","dc:identifier":"homoit0002901","skos:prefLabel":"Asian asexual people","skos:altLabel":["Asexual Asian people","Asexual Asians"],"rdfs:comment":"Asexual individuals of Asian heritage or descent, living in Asia or part of the Asian diaspora. Only use for people who self-identify as Asian.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000074","skos:related":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002902","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000915"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-29"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002902","dc:identifier":"homoit0002902","skos:prefLabel":"Asian American asexual people","skos:altLabel":["Asexual Asian American people","Asexual Asian Americans"],"rdfs:comment":"Asexual people of Asian heritage or descent born in the United States or born in Asian countries who live in or have a strong connection to the United States. Only use for individuals who self-identify as Asian American.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000073","skos:related":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002901","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000915"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-29"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002903","dc:identifier":"homoit0002903","skos:prefLabel":"Multiracial asexual people","skos:altLabel":["Asexual multi-racial people","Asexual multiracial people","Multi-racial asexual people"],"rdfs:comment":"Asexual people who belong to two or more racial groups.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000903","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000915"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-29"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002904","dc:identifier":"homoit0002904","skos:prefLabel":"Biracial asexual people","skos:altLabel":["Asexual bi-racial people","Asexual biracial people","Bi-racial asexual people"],"rdfs:comment":"Asexual people who belong to two or more racial groups. Only use to describe people who self-identify as biracial.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000820","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000915"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-29"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002905","dc:identifier":"homoit0002905","skos:prefLabel":"Closeted asexual people","rdfs:comment":"Asexual people who do not identify as such to others.","skos:broader":["https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000071","https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001783"],"skos:related":["https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000285","https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000286","https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000287","https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000295","https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001065","https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001261","https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001784","https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001785","https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001786"],"skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001297"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-29"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002906","dc:identifier":"homoit0002906","skos:prefLabel":"Daughters of asexual people","rdfs:comment":"Daughters who have one or more asexual parent.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002352","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000421"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-29"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002907","dc:identifier":"homoit0002907","skos:prefLabel":"Fathers of asexual people","rdfs:comment":"People who assume the role of father to asexual people. Only use to describe individuals who identify as fathers.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002342","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000421"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-29"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002908","dc:identifier":"homoit0002908","skos:prefLabel":"Jewish asexual people","skos:altLabel":"Asexual Jews","rdfs:comment":"Asexual people who are Jewish.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000682","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000915"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-29"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002909","dc:identifier":"homoit0002909","skos:prefLabel":"Mothers of asexual people","rdfs:comment":"A person who assumes the role of mother to an asexual person; use only for individuals who self-identify as mothers.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002348","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000421"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-29"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002910","dc:identifier":"homoit0002910","skos:prefLabel":"Parents of asexual people","skos:altLabel":"Asexual people's parents","rdfs:comment":"People who act as parents to one or more asexual person. For parents who are themselves asexual, use the term \"Asexual parents.\"","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002224","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000421"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-29"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002911","dc:identifier":"homoit0002911","skos:prefLabel":"Partners of asexual people","rdfs:comment":"People whose sexual, romantic, or play partner is asexual.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002366","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002365"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-29"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002912","dc:identifier":"homoit0002912","skos:prefLabel":"Persecution of asexual people","rdfs:comment":"Systematic mistreatment of asexual people on the basis of their sexual identity.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001108","skos:related":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000071","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001108"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-29"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002913","dc:identifier":"homoit0002913","skos:prefLabel":"Romani asexual people","skos:altLabel":["Asexual Roma","Asexual Romanis","Asexual Romany","Roma asexual people","Romany asexual people"],"rdfs:comment":"Asexual people who are Romani, or part of the traditionally nomadic Indo-European people.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000875","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000915"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-29"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002914","dc:identifier":"homoit0002914","skos:prefLabel":"Sons of asexual people","rdfs:comment":"Sons who have one or more asexual parent. For sons who are themselves asexual, use the term \"Asexual sons.\"","skos:broader":["https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001766","https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002355"],"skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000421"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-29"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002915","dc:identifier":"homoit0002915","skos:prefLabel":"White asexual people","skos:altLabel":["Asexual Caucasian people","Asexual white people"],"rdfs:comment":"Asexual people who are white.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000243","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000243"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-29"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002916","dc:identifier":"homoit0002916","skos:prefLabel":"Intersex mothers","rdfs:comment":"Intersex people who are mothers. Only use to describe individuals who identify as mothers.","skos:broader":["https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001021","https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001763","https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002343"],"skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000421"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-30"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002917","dc:identifier":"homoit0002917","skos:prefLabel":"Intersex museums","rdfs:comment":"Institutions dedicated to the storage, exhibition, conservation, and preservation of materials relating to intersex people and intersex history.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000907","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001755"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-30"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002918","dc:identifier":"homoit0002918","skos:prefLabel":"Intersex newsletters","rdfs:comment":"Serialized print or digital periodicals created by or for intersex people, often with the purpose of sharing news and information related to intersex communities and intersex issues.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001041","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001132"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-30"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002919","dc:identifier":"homoit0002919","skos:prefLabel":"Intersex newspapers","rdfs:comment":"Serialized print or digital publications about intersex issues (and typically produced by intersex people or intersex organizations) that contain news, articles, columns, correspondence, and advertisements.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001042","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001132"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-30"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002920","dc:identifier":"homoit0002920","skos:prefLabel":"Intersex partners","rdfs:comment":"Intersex people who are the romantic, sexual, or play partners of another person or people.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001083","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000420"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-30"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002921","dc:identifier":"homoit0002921","skos:prefLabel":"Intersex people in the military","rdfs:comment":"Intersex people serving in state and national armed forces.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001100","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000712"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-30"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002922","dc:identifier":"homoit0002922","skos:prefLabel":"Indigenous asexual people","skos:altLabel":"Asexual Indigenous people","rdfs:comment":"Asexual people who are part of an Indigenous people, or culturally distinct ethnic group whose members are directly descended from the earliest known inhabitants of a particular region and, to some extent, maintain the language and culture of those people. Only use to describe people who identify as Indigenous; use more specific terms whenever possible.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000886","skos:narrower":["https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002923","https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002924","https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002925","https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002926","https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002927"],"skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000915"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-30"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002923","dc:identifier":"homoit0002923","skos:prefLabel":"Inuit asexual people","rdfs:comment":"Asexual people who are Inuit, or part of several Indigenous peoples inhabiting the Inuit homeland in the Arctic and subarctic regions of Greenland, Canada, and Alaska. Only use for people who identify as Inuit.","skos:broader":["https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000676","https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002922"],"skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000915"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-30"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002924","dc:identifier":"homoit0002924","skos:prefLabel":"Kānaka Maoli asexual people","skos:altLabel":["Asexual Indigenous Hawaiians","Asexual Kānaka Maoli","Asexual Kānaka ʻŌiwi","Asexual Native Hawaiians","Indigenous Hawaiian asexual people","Native Hawaiian asexual people"],"rdfs:comment":"Asexual people who are part of the Indigenous people of Mokupuni o Hawai‘i, or the Hawaiian Islands; only use to refer to people who identify as Kanaka Maoli.","skos:broader":["https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001955","https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002922"],"skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000915"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-30"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002925","dc:identifier":"homoit0002925","skos:prefLabel":"Native American asexual people","skos:altLabel":["American Indian asexual people","Asexual American Indians","Asexual Native Americans"],"rdfs:comment":"People who are part of the Native peoples of the continental United States and Canada (excluding Inuit people) and self-identity as asexual. Use more specific terms when available.","skos:broader":["https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001037","https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002922"],"skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000915"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-30"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002926","dc:identifier":"homoit0002926","skos:prefLabel":"Pacific Islander American asexual people","skos:altLabel":"Pacific Islander asexual people","rdfs:comment":"Asexual people who identify as both Pacific Islanders (or of Pacific Islander ancestry) and American.","skos:broader":["https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001812","https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002922"],"skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000915"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-30"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002927","dc:identifier":"homoit0002927","skos:prefLabel":"Papuan asexual people","skos:altLabel":["Asexual Papuan people","Asexual Papuans"],"rdfs:comment":"Asexual people who are Papuan, or part of the Indigenous peoples of new Guinea; only use for people who identify as Papuan.","skos:broader":["https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000913","https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002922"],"skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000915"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-30"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002928","dc:identifier":"homoit0002928","skos:prefLabel":"Intersex people with chronic illnesses","skos:altLabel":"Chronically ill intersex people","rdfs:comment":"Intersex people who have long-term health conditions that require continued medical attention and/or significantly impact or limit daily life; some but not all people with chronic illnesses consider themselves disabled.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001879","skos:related":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002929","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000915"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-30"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002929","dc:identifier":"homoit0002929","skos:prefLabel":"Intersex disabled people","rdfs:comment":"Intersex people who are disabled.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001854","skos:narrower":["https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002932","https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002952"],"skos:related":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002928","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000915"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-30"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002930","dc:identifier":"homoit0002930","skos:prefLabel":"Intersex Deaf people","skos:altLabel":"Deaf intersex people","rdfs:comment":"Intersex people with significant or total hearing loss. Some but not all Deaf people prefer to capitalize Deaf in recognition of the cultural and linguistic identity they share with other Deaf people.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000337","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000337"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-30"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002931","dc:identifier":"homoit0002931","skos:prefLabel":"Intersex community","rdfs:comment":"Groups of people who share the experience of being intersex.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000297","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000297"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-30"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002932","dc:identifier":"homoit0002932","skos:prefLabel":"Intersex blind people","skos:altLabel":"Blind intersex people","rdfs:comment":"Intersex people with significant or total loss of vision.","skos:broader":["https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000822","https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002929"],"skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000915"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-30"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002933","dc:identifier":"homoit0002933","skos:prefLabel":"Intersex political activists","rdfs:comment":"Intersex people working to bring about political or social change through public organizing.","skos:broader":["https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000007","https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002751"],"skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000915"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-30"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002934","dc:identifier":"homoit0002934","skos:prefLabel":"Intersex studies","rdfs:comment":"Academic field of research and study about intersex people, intersex identity, and intersex communities.","skos:related":["https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000579","https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001210"],"skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002934"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-30"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002935","dc:identifier":"homoit0002935","skos:prefLabel":"African American intersex people","skos:altLabel":["Intersex African American people","Intersex African Americans"],"rdfs:comment":"Intersex people of African descent born in the United States or intersex people born in African countries who live in or have a strong connection to the United States. Use for individuals who self-identity as African American. Follow the individual’s preference for African American or Black whenever possible.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000015","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000915"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-30"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002936","dc:identifier":"homoit0002936","skos:prefLabel":"Intersex press","rdfs:comment":"Newspapers, magazines, or other publishing outlets aimed at intersex audiences and/or operated by intersex people.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001132","skos:narrower":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0003148","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001132"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-09-29"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002937","dc:identifier":"homoit0002937","skos:prefLabel":"Intersex publishers","rdfs:comment":"Companies and organizations that publish materials for intersex communities or addressing intersex issues.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001144","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001144"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-09-29"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002938","dc:identifier":"homoit0002938","skos:prefLabel":"Intersex radio","rdfs:comment":"Auditory content broadcast on the radio (analog or digital) about intersex people and intersex issues.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001231","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001231"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-09-29"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002939","dc:identifier":"homoit0002939","skos:prefLabel":"Intersex relationships","rdfs:comment":"Ongoing connections (romantic, sexual, or platonic) between two or more intersex people.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001237","skos:narrower":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002969","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001237"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-09-29"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002940","dc:identifier":"homoit0002940","skos:prefLabel":"Intersex slang","rdfs:comment":"Colloquial or informal language related to intersex identity or used by intersex people.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001317","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0001317"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-09-29"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002941","dc:identifier":"homoit0002941","skos:prefLabel":"Intersex sons","rdfs:comment":"Intersex people who are sons. Only use to describe individuals who identify as sons.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001329","skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0000915"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-09-29"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2025-02-23"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"},"skos:changeNote":"Version 3.7.0"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v4/homoit0002942","dc:identifier":"homoit0002942","skos:prefLabel":"Intersex survivors of hate crimes","rdfs:comment":"Intersex people who have experienced and survived hate crimes; follow the individual's preference for \"victims\" or \"survivors\" whenever possible.","skos:broader":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001349","skos:hasT